# Scope

A simple mediation model
will be used as an example. This model
is trivial, but is simple enough to
illustrate how to use `semeqmodels`.

# Original Model

Suppose this is the original model we fitted
to the dataset `data_test_3obvs`
(installed with the package):

```{r}
library(semeqmodels)
round(head(data_test_3obvs), 2)
```

```{r demo_obs_3_model, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "Simple Mediation Model"}
library(semeqmodels)
library(semPlot)
suppressMessages(library(lavaan))
mod <-
"
fm ~ fx
fy ~ fm + fx
"
fit0 <- sem(mod, data_test_3obvs, do.fit = FALSE)
layout_m <- matrix(c(NA, "fm", NA,
                     "fx", NA, "fy"), 2, 3, byrow = TRUE)
p <- semPaths(fit0,
              layout = layout_m,
              nCharNodes = 0,
              exoCov = FALSE,
              residuals = FALSE,
              sizeMan = 10,
              asize = 5,
              DoNotPlot = TRUE)
plot(p)
```

This is the model:

```{r}
mod <-
"
fm ~ fx
fy ~ fm + fx
"
```

This is the lavaan results for the model:

```{r}
library(lavaan)
fit <- sem(
  model = mod,
  data = data_test_3obvs,
  fixed.x = FALSE
)
fit
```

